     "page": 1,
     "text": "JIMENEZ EDWARDS AND WATSON CLINIC - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: Gregg Clark DOB: 03/06/1968 Sex: M MRN: 535769\nAdmit: 05/19/2022 Discharge: 08/27/2025\n\nPrincipal diagnosis: HIV (on ART 9.8 years; VL undetectable for 17 months; CD4 count 637; AIDS-defining illness)\n\nSecondary diagnosis: None\n\nHospital course:\nMr. Clark, a 57-year-old male with a history of HIV, was admitted for routine monitoring and management of his HIV status. He has been on therapy (ART) for 9.8 years and his viral load has been undetectable for the past 17 months. His most recent CD4 count is 637 cells/mm³, indicating a stable immune status.\n\nDuring his hospital stay, Mr. Clark underwent a comprehensive evaluation including physical examination, laboratory tests, and review of his current medication regimen. His vital signs were stable throughout his admission with a blood pressure of 124/76 mmHg and a weight of 190 pounds on the last visit. physical examination was unremarkable with no acute distress noted. His cardiovascular, respiratory, and abdominal examinations were within normal limits. There were no signs of opportunistic infections or other AIDS-defining conditions. \n\nLaboratory tests confirmed his HIV status with a CD4 count of 637 cells/mm³ and an undetectable viral load. His liver function tests, renal function tests, and complete blood count were all within normal ranges. \n\nMr. Clark continued to take his ART medication, bictegravir-emtricitabine-tenofovir 50/200/25 mg, without any reported side effects. He denied any new symptoms or complaints during his stay. \n\nSocial history reveals that Mr. Clark consumes approximately one alcoholic drink per week. He denies tobacco use. His family history is non-contributory.\n\nProcedures: None during this admission.\n\nDischarge medications:\n1. bictegravir-emtricitabine-tenofovir 50/200/25 mg PO daily\n\nDischarge plan:\nMr. Clark was discharged home in stable condition. He was advised to continue his current ART regimen and maintain regular follow-up appointments with his primary care provider and the HIV clinic. He was instructed to return to the clinic if he experiences any new symptoms or if his condition changes. \n\nFollow-up:\n- Rheumatology follow-up with Dr. Rachel Murphy in 3 months for re-evaluation of his HIV status and ART efficacy.\n- Primary care follow-up in months for general health maintenance and routine screenings.\n\nPatient education:\nMr. Clark was educated on the importance of adherence to his ART regimen, the significance of regular follow-up appointments, and the need to monitor"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "for any signs of opportunistic infections or other complications related to HIV. He was also counseled on the importance of a healthy lifestyle, including diet, exercise, and limited alcohol consumption.\n\nSignature:\nDr. Rachel Murphy\nRheumatologist\nJimenez Edwards and Watson Clinic\n\nPage 1 of 4\n\nJIMENEZ EDWARDS AND WATSON CLINIC - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: Gregg Clark DOB: 03/06/1968 Sex: M MRN: 535769\nAdmit: 05/19/2022 Discharge: 08/27/2025\n\nContinued from page 1:\n\nDetailed hospital course:\nMr. Clark's hospital course was uneventful. He remained hemodynamically stable throughout his stay with no episodes of fever, chills, or night sweats. His gastrointestinal symptoms were minimal, consisting only of occasional mild bloating, which he attributed to his diet. There were no signs of hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy. \n\nHis neurological examination was normal with no focal deficits. His musculoskeletal examination revealed no joint pain or swelling. His skin examination showed no rashes or lesions. \n\nMr. Clark's mental status examination was normal with no signs of cognitive impairment or mood disorders. He was alert and oriented to person, place, and time. His speech was clear and coherent. \n\nLaboratory results:\n- CD4 count: 637 cells/mm³\n- Viral load: Undetectable\n- Complete blood count: Within normal limits\n- Comprehensive metabolic panel: Within normal limits\n- Liver function tests: Within normal limits\n- Renal function tests: Within normal limits\n\nImaging studies:\nNo imaging studies were required during this admission as Mr. Clark's clinical presentation and laboratory results were consistent with a stable HIV status.\n\nDischarge instructions:\nMr. Clark was provided with detailed discharge instructions, including a summary of his hospital course, discharge medications, and follow-up plans. He was instructed to take his ART medication as prescribed and to avoid missing any doses. He was advised to maintain a healthy diet, engage in regular physical activity, and limit his alcohol consumption to no more than one drink per week. \n\nHe was also instructed to seek immediate medical attention if he experiences any new symptoms, including fever, chills, night sweats, weight loss, or any signs of infection. He was reminded of the importance of regular follow-up appointments with his primary care provider and the HIV clinic.\n\nPage 2 of 4\n\nJIMENEZ EDWARDS AND WATSON CLINIC - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: Gregg Clark DOB: Sex: M MRN: 535769\nAdmit: 05/79/2022 Discharge: 08/27/2025\n\nContinued from page 2:\n\nAdditional notes:\nMr. Clark'"
